  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Three samples of the same gas <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>&nbsp;(<mi></mi><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>) have equal volume initially. Now, the volume of each sample is doubled. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>, the process is adiabatic; for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>, it is isobaric and for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>, the process is isothermal. If the final pressures are equal for all the three samples, the ratio of their initial pressures is</p>\r",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "WZ2Sf",
      "text": "<math> <mrow> <mn>2</mn> <mtext>&nbsp;:&nbsp;</mtext> <mn>1</mn> <mtext>&nbsp;:&nbsp;</mtext> <msqrt> <mn>2</mn> </msqrt> </mrow> </math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
